= Sampling by poisson point processes
We now move our attention to sampling algorithms based on a Poisson point process.

Let $x$ be a non-negative#footnote[so that it makes sense as a rate of a point process; we show a
slight generalization of this later.] real random variable.

== Algorithm derivation
@Andrea states the algorithm, here show how to derive it.
#meta-prop[
Let $pr(Y,t)$ be a process that conditionally on $x$ is distributed as a Poisson point process of
rate $x$, in the sense described in @poisson-mix on #ref(<poisson-mix>, form: "page"). We denote this predicate from now on by $ (Y|x) ~ "PPP"(x). $
This means that the jump probabilities of $Y$ are required to satisfy 

#eq($ forall t>=0, delta>0: PP(Y_(t+delta) thin &= Yt + 1 | x) = delta x + o(delta) \ "and" PP(Y_(t+delta) thin &= Yt | x) = 1 - delta x + o(delta), $)<poisson-prob>

i.e. the probability of a tick occurring (a jump of the counting variable $Yt$) in any time interval $(t,t+delta)$ is $delta x$ to the
first order in $delta$.
]

#derivation(suffix: none)[
Note that these are equations of almost-everywhere defined functions of $x$, since we condition on $x$
in the probabilities. As time progresses from $t=0$ on, though, treating $x$ as latent (not
measurable at $t$), we only observe the history of $Y_t$; i.e. as far as the performer of the
algorithm is concerned, the jump probabilities he or she observes are the expected values of @poisson-prob given $Y_t$:

$ EE[PP(Y_(t+delta) thin &= Yt + 1 | x) | Y_t] = delta thin EE[x given Y_t] + o(delta). $

#let Jh = $hat(J)_(t,delta)$
Denote the jump event by $Jh := (Y_(t+delta) = Yt + 1)$. Now, the $sigma$-algebra generated by $x$ is no rougher than $Y_t$ at any time, so expected probability of the event of a jump #Jh on the left is simply
$ EE[PP(Jh | x) given Y_t] &= EE[EE[1_Jh | x] | Y_t] \ &=^! EE[1_Jh | Y_t] = PP(Jh | Y_t), $
so the transition probabilities of $Y_t$ are
$      PP(Y_(t+delta) thin &= Yt + 1 | Y_t) = delta thin EE[x given Yt] + o(delta)
\ "and" PP(Y_(t+delta) thin &= Yt | Yt) = 1 - delta thin EE[x given Yt] + o(delta). #derivation-end-here $
]

Putting $m(t,y) = EE[x given Y_t = y]$ (for each $t$, an almost surely defined function of $y$), this
gives 

#conclusion[*An algorithm for sampling $Y$*:

Discretise time into $t_0 = 0, delta, 2 delta, ..., M delta$. Iterating over $k$:
#grid(
  columns: 2,
  align: (col,row) => if col == 0 { right } else { left },
  inset: .5em,
  [At $t=0$,],
  grid.vline(stroke: (dash: "densely-dotted", paint: colour.fg4)),
  [let $y := 0.$],
  grid.hline(stroke: (dash: "densely-dotted", paint: colour.fg4)),
  [At each\ $t=k delta$,],
  [increment $y <- (y + 1)$ with probability $delta dot.c m(t,Y_t)$, otherwise keep $Y_t$ the same.]
)
The rate $x$ of $Y_t$ that the algorithm recovers this way at time $T$, i.e. the sample $x$ that we output, is then $ x = y / T. $
]

Of course, here we need to be able to evaluate $m(t,y)$, as we mentioned in the first section.

== Correctness
#meta-prop[
Let again $x>=0$ and $(Y | x) ~ "PPP"(x)$.
Now we prove $ lim_t Y_t / t = x "(almost surely)". $
]
#derivation(suffix:none)[
  #let counting = footnote[If for $i in NN$, $A_i$ are events, we put $\#_i A_i$ to be the random variable counting how many of them occured: $\#_i A_i := sum_i 1_(A_i)$]
The counting variable $Y_t$ of a ticking (point) process is the number#counting of inter-arrival times $xi_i, i in NN$ that occur up to time $t$:
$ Y_t = limits(\#)_i (xi_i < t), $
and the $n$th arrival time is the sum
$ T_n = sum_(i = 1)^n xi_i. $

Any time $t>=0$ then lies between two consecutive arrival times#footnote[as long as the process does
not stop in finite time.]:
#eq($ forall t,n: quad sum_(n <= Y_t) xi_n <= t < sum_(n <= Yt + 1) xi_n. $)<squeeze-time>

For our poisson point proccess all $xi_i$ are independent and exponentially distributed of parameter
$x$. By the law or large numbers then the average of $xi_i$ converges almost surely to the mean,
$1\/x$. That is, #eq($ lim_t (limits(sum)_(n <= Y_t) xi_n)/Y_t = 1/x. $)<exp-distr-limit>
Divide @squeeze-time by $Y_t$ then to squeeze $t/Y_t$ inside $1/x$:
$ (limits(sum)_(n <= Y_t) xi_n)/Y_t <= t/Y_t &< (limits(sum)_(n <= Yt + 1) xi_n)/Yt 
                                           \ &= (limits(sum)_(n <= Yt + 1) xi_n)/(Yt+1) dot (Yt+1)/Yt, $
apply the squeeze lemma and @exp-distr-limit to the limit as $t -> infinity$:
$ 1/x <= lim_t t/Yt <= 1/x, $
proving that $ #derivation-end-here Yt / t = x. $
]
== A speedup of $g(t)$
Here we're generally interested in what happens if instead of running a process $(Y | x) ~ "PPP"(x)$
of rate $x$, we use a rate#footnote[again constrained nonnegative] $f(t,x)$  and run $(Y^((f)) | x) ~ "PPP"(t mapsto f(x,t)).$

#meta-prop[
We first consider the special case $f(t,x)=g(t) thin x$ and a process $ (Y^((g)) | x) ~ "PPP"(t mapsto g(t) thin x), $
for a positive function $g(t)>0$.
]

At each $omega in Omega$, $Y(omega)$ behaves like a Poisson point process of rate $g(t) x(omega)$.
Compared to our canonical $(Y | x) ~ "PPP"(x)$, this means that a global (over $x$'s values)
time-speedup of $g(t)$ has been applied.

What does this mean in terms of our sampling algorithm? Can it still produce a sample of $x$ if it
runs $Y^((g))$ instead of $Y$?

#derivation(suffix:none)[
For the canonical $Y$ with rate $x$, $Y_t$
is a Poisson random variable of rate $t thin x$. The modified $Y^((g))$ consists of Poisson random
variables of rate $integral_0^t g(s) x d s$: 
$ \(Y^((g))_t | x) ~ "Poisson"(G(t) thin x), quad "for" G(t)=integral_0^t g(s) d s $

If we can find a change of time $tau=tau(t)$ such that $Y^((g))_tau(t)$ is distributed as a Poisson
variable of rate $t thin x$, so that $\(Y^((g))_tau(t))_t | x ~ "PPP"(x)$, then the correctness proof applies
and $Y^((g))_tau(t) \/ t$ will approach the sample $x$.

Indeed, to find a time $tau$ such that the Poisson parameter $G(t) thin x$ turns out to be $t thin
x$, means to find $tau$ such that $G(tau(t)) = t$. In other words - $tau$ must be an inverse of $G$.
As an integral of a positive function $g$, $G$ is continuous and monotonic and hence invertible on
its range $[0,G(T)]$, as long as $T$ is picked so that $G(T)$ is finite.

#let no-proof-poisson = footnote[we do not prove that the time-rescaled Poisson variables $Y^((g))_tau$ indeed induce a Poisson point process]

Now assume $G(t)$ is invertible with inverse $tau$, so that $ (Y^((g))_tau(t) | x) ~ "Poisson"(t x) $
and the rescaled back process $\(Y^((g))_tau(t))_t$ is#no-proof-poisson a Poisson point process of rate
$x$. Then by the correctness proof, it induces a sample-approximating sequence:
$ Y^((g))_(tau(t)) / t stretch(->)_(t->infinity) x. $
However, we are interested in the behaviour not of the normalised $\(Y^((g))_(tau(t)))_t$, but of the sped-up $Y^((g))$.
To get that, we change variables, $t = G(tau(t))$:

$ x stretch(<-)_(t->infinity) Y^((g))_(tau(t)) / t = Y^((g))_tau(t) / G(tau(t)),
\ #derivation-end-here "and so" Y^((g))_tau / G(tau) stretch(->)_(tau->infinity) x $
]

#conclusion[
If $g$ is a positive integrable function of time, then a sample $x$ can be approximated by
$ Y^((g))_tau/G(tau),quad "where" quad #align(left, stack(spacing: 1em, $(Y^((g)) | x) ~ "PPP"(t mapsto g(t)x)$, $"and" G(tau) = integral_0^tau g(t) dt$)) $
]

== Speedup measurement
The speedup factor $g$ discussed above makes the point process tick $g(t)$ times more often. This
makes us expect that there will be an improvement in the approximation $Y^((g))_tau\/G(tau)$ at a fixed time $T$.

#let eps-expand = footnote[$A^epsilon := {y given exists y' in A : |y-y'|<epsilon }$]

#meta-prop[
  Fix again a $g>0$, put $G(t) = integral_0^t g$ and pick a boosted process $ \(Y^((g)) | x) ~ "PPP"(t mapsto g(t) x). $

  Pick $T>0$ and define the Prokhorov distance between the laws of the target $x$ and the approximating sample $X_T := Y^((g))_T\/G(T)$ 
  to be the infimum of $ { epsilon > 0 given forall A in calB: 
  #stack(spacing:1em, $PP(X_T in A) <= PP(x in A^epsilon) + epsilon$,
      $"and" PP(x in A) <= PP(X_T in A^epsilon) + epsilon$) }, $
      and $A^epsilon$ denotes the $epsilon$-expansion#eps-expand of any $A in calB(RR)$.

      $ pi_T := inf P_T,$ where $P_T$ denotes the set

  The question is, can we bound $pi_T$?
]

#derivation(suffix:none)[
In @ConvergenceBook we find a statement of a theorem by Strassen-Dudley, whose converse allows us to rework the Prohorov distance $pi_T$. 
In particular, we consider the following decomposition of the event $X_T in A$:
$ X_T in A subset.eq (|x - X_T| > epsilon) union (x in A^epsilon). $
It is easiest to visualise what this means if we draw the product space $RR times.o RR$ and treat
the two axes as the axes of values of the random variables $x$ and $X_T$; then the inclusion above
is a statement about areas in $RR^2$.

The inclusion implies a respective inequality about probabilities,
#eq($ PP(X_T in A) <= PP(|x - X_T| > epsilon) + PP(x in A^epsilon). $)<strassen>

Now _if_ we find an $epsilon>0$ that keeps the left summand less than $epsilon$, 
so satisfies #eq($ PP(|x-X_T|>epsilon)<epsilon, $)<markov-like>
we will get the predicate in the infinmum defining $pi_T$:
#eq($ PP(X_T in A) <= epsilon + PP(x in A^epsilon). $)<prokhorov-ineq>

Thus in our goal of bounding $pi_T$, where we have to find an $epsilon>0$ satisfying @prokhorov-ineq
(the smaller, the better), we can search instead for $epsilon>0$ satisfying simply @markov-like.

// #let dscb = footnote[$PP(Y^((g))_T >= G(T) epsilon + x G(T)) = jkj$]

@markov-like, however, means $ PP(|x - Y^((g))_T \/ G(T)| > epsilon) < epsilon, $
and we can condition on $x$ to apply Poisson'ness of $Y^((g))_T$:
$ PP&(|x - Y^((g))_T \/ G(T)| > epsilon) 
\ &= EE[PP(|x - Y^((g))_T \/ G(T)| > epsilon given x)] 
                                      // \ &= EE[PP(|Y^((g))_T - G(T) x| > G(T) epsilon given x)] 
\ &#[(by the double-sided Chernoff bound#footnote[the only place in this thesis we have not thoroughly verified an LLM's output. Hopefully I will find time to write this out in full detail in the near future.])]
\                                       &<=2 thin EE[exp(-G(T) thin h(epsilon\/x) thin x)] \ &wide "for" h(u)=(1+u)ln(1+u)
                                      \ &<=2 thin EE[exp(-(G(T)epsilon^2)/(2(x+epsilon/3)))
                                      #derivation-end-here $
]

#conclusion[
As a bound on the distance $pi_T$ we can take the solution of the equation
$ 2 epsilon = EE[exp(-(G(T)epsilon^2)/(2(x+epsilon/3)))], $
which cannot be solved analytically in general.
]

== If $x$ is not nonnegative
#let sign=math.op("sign")
The rate of a point process naturally must be nonnegative, but we can also ask if this framework is
still useful for some not necessarily nonnegative random variables $x$. For example, if we decompose $x = sign(x) dot |x|$, we can apply the machinery above with $|x|$. Then, if $sign(x)$ and $|x|$ are independent, we can sample separately $|x|$ by the algorithm above, sample $epsilon.alt ~ sign(x)$, and then emit $epsilon.alt thin Y_T\/T$. A natural question then is, under what conditions are $sign(x)$ and $|x|$ independent?

Turns out, we can prove the following equivalence: 
$     &(|x| perp sign(x) | x != 0) #flushr[(I)]
\ <=> &PP(x given x>0) = PP(-x given x<0), #flushr[(L)] $

which in words reads as the equivalence of the following:
+ (I) Given $x != 0$, $|x|$ and $sign(x)$ are independent;
+ (L) The conditional law of $x$ given $(x>0)$ is equal to the conditional law of
  $-x$ given $(x<0)$.

That is, the sign is independent from the modulus exactly when $x$'s law, normalised on $x>0$,
coincides with its negative's law normalised on $x<0$. 
We condition on $x!=0$ to allow cases with non-zero probability for the event $(x=0)$. 

#proof(of: " " + $(==>)$)[
The forward direction is direct, as the following chain of equalities of the laws of $x,-x,|x|$ shows:
$ PP(x | x>0) &= PP(|x| given x>0)
            \ &=^perp P(|x| given x != 0) #flushl[(Assumed (I))]
               =^perp PP(|x| given x<0)   
            \ &= PP(-x given x<0). $
]

#proof(of: $(<==)$)[
#let eps= math.epsilon.alt
The converse relies on Bayes' formula. 

Assuming temporarily that $(x = 0)$ happens almost never, and the equality of laws (L), we have that for both $eps in {-1,+1}$ and all Borel $A$,
$ PP(|x| in A) &= PP(eps x in A, eps x >0) + PP(-eps x in A, eps x < 0)
\ #flushl[(Bayes)]
 &= PP(eps x in A given eps x >0) med PP(eps x >0) \ & wide op(+) PP(-eps x in A given eps x < 0) med PP(eps x < 0)
\ &= PP(eps x in A given eps x >0) med PP(eps x >0) 
\ #flushl[(L)]& wide op(+) PP(eps x in A given eps x > 0) med PP(eps x < 0)
\ #flushl[(grouping)] &= PP(eps x in A given eps x >0) [ PP(eps x >0) + PP(eps x < 0) ]
\ #flushl[$(x != 0)$] &= PP(eps x in A given eps x >0)
\ &= PP(|x| in A given sign(x)= eps).
$

Without the assumption that $(x=0)$ almost doesn't happen, the equality chain still holds if we
condition everything on $(x!=0)$#footnote[and observe that $(sign(x)=eps) subset (x!=0)$, because $eps != 0$.]:
$ PP(|x| in A | x != 0) = PP(|x| in A given sign(x)= eps). $
But this means exactly that the conditional law of $|x|$ (on $sign(x)$) equals the unconditional, i.e. $|x|$ is independent from $sign(x)$.
]

Hence, if $x$ has such a symmetric law, we can still use the Poisson point process based algorithm
to sample it (if we can also sample its sign).


